<Layout
  title="Ironwood - Hardware - Rycode"
  description="Ironwood is Google's seventh-generation TPU, listed at $12 per chip-hour on demand and $5.40 on a three-year commitment in us-central1. Trillium (TPU v6e) is the generation before it."
  variant="ai"
>
  <article class="ai-model">
    <p class="ai-model-kicker">Hardware · Chips</p>
    <h1 class="ai-model-title">Ironwood</h1>
    <p class="ai-model-dek">Ironwood is Google's seventh-generation TPU. A TPU, or Tensor Processing Unit, is the chip Google designs for its own AI work and also rents to other companies through Google Cloud. Google Cloud calls it TPU7x and says it handles both training and inference (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/release-notes">Google Cloud, Cloud TPU release notes</a>). Training is teaching a model. Inference is running it to answer requests. Ironwood went into Preview on 24 November 2025 and became generally available on 31 March 2026. <a href="#trillium">Trillium</a>, the sixth-generation TPU that came before it, is further down this page.</p>
    <ChipFigure src="/ai/hardware/chips/ironwood.jpg" alt="Google Ironwood TPU: a metal-framed chip with amber silicon and the word Ironwood printed on the frame, on a black background." caption="Google Ironwood (TPU7x)." credit="Image: Google" />
    <hr class="ai-model-rule" />

    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Ironwood superpod</h2>
    <p>Google says Ironwood scales up to 9,216 chips in a superpod, linked by its Inter-Chip Interconnect network (<a href="https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/compute/ironwood-tpus-and-new-axion-based-vms-for-your-ai-workloads">Google Cloud, Ironwood TPUs and new Axion-based VMs</a>).</p>
    <ChipFigure src="/ai/hardware/chips/ironwood-rack.jpg" alt="Wide-angle photo of a curved wall of Ironwood TPU racks packed with blue and yellow cables, under yellow overhead cable trays in a Google data center." caption="Part of an Ironwood superpod." credit="Image: Google" variant="rack" />

    <h2 class="ai-model-section">What Google charges</h2>
    <p>Google lists Ironwood prices per chip-hour, which is one chip rented for one hour (<a href="https://cloud.google.com/tpu/pricing">Google Cloud, Cloud TPU pricing</a>). The page carries no publish date.</p>
    <div class="ai-model-table-wrap">
      <table class="ai-model-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Ironwood, us-central1 (Iowa)</th>
            <th scope="col">Per chip-hour</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>On demand</td><td>$12.00</td></tr>
          <tr><td>DWS Flex-start</td><td>$6.00</td></tr>
          <tr><td>DWS Calendar mode</td><td>$8.40</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1-year commitment</td><td>$8.40</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3-year commitment</td><td>$5.40</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>On demand means you pay by the hour with no promise to keep using it. A commitment means you agree to pay for one or three years in return for a lower rate. Flex-start and Calendar mode are two ways to get chips through Google's Dynamic Workload Scheduler, a booking system for TPU capacity. Calendar mode reserves chips for set future dates. In London (europe-west2), on demand is $13.20 per chip-hour.</p>
    <p>So the $12 figure is the on-demand price. With a commitment, the same chip-hour costs $8.40 for one year or $5.40 for three.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">What an outside test found</h2>
    <p>SemiAnalysis, a chip research firm, runs InferenceX, a public test of what it costs to serve AI models on different chips. On 7 September 2026 it published the first outside inference results for Ironwood (<a href="https://inferencex.semianalysis.com/blog/tpu-inferencex-full-steam">SemiAnalysis, TPU Inference Externalization Full Steam Ahead</a>).</p>
    <p>The test setup was narrow. It ran one open-weight model, Qwen3.5 397B, which means anyone can download and run it. Both sides used FP8, a number format that stores each value in 8 bits, which is faster and cheaper than larger formats. Prompts were about 8,000 tokens in and 1,000 out, where a token is a small chunk of text. Serving was aggregated, meaning the same chips read the prompt and write the reply.</p>
    <p>The main results compare Ironwood with Nvidia's B200 and B300 GPUs:</p>
    <div class="ai-model-table-wrap">
      <table class="ai-model-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Test point</th>
            <th scope="col">Ironwood</th>
            <th scope="col">B200</th>
            <th scope="col">B300</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Cost per million tokens at 100 tokens per second per user</td><td>about $0.181</td><td>$0.222</td><td>$0.276</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Tokens per second per user is how fast each person sees the reply appear. At that speed, SemiAnalysis says Ironwood is "approximately 19% lower cost than B200." At a slower 20 tokens per second per user, Ironwood delivered 50.4% more tokens per dollar than B200. Measured by total response time, Ironwood was about 8% cheaper than B200 at a 20-second median reply. Around a 30-second median reply, B200 can come out ahead.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">What the result does not show</h2>
    <p>These are not rental prices. The costs use SemiAnalysis's modeled total cost of ownership for a large buyer that purchases TPUs. Total cost of ownership, or TCO, is what it costs to buy, house, power, and run a chip over its life, spread out per hour. It is not Google's $12 list price.</p>
    <p>Google's own cost is a different number. SemiAnalysis estimates Google's internal TCO at $1.03 per chip-hour and uses it for two separate comparisons: a high-load point against B200 and B300, and the GB300 NVL72 comparison below. It is not the cost behind the 19% result.</p>
    <p>Ironwood has no native FP4, a smaller 4-bit format that Nvidia GPUs run, and SemiAnalysis says Nvidia GPUs still lead on FP4.</p>
    <p>Nvidia's rack setup wins part of the range. Against Nvidia's <a href="/ai/hardware/chips/blackwell/">GB300 NVL72</a> rack running disaggregated serving, where separate chips read the prompt and write the reply, SemiAnalysis found about a 30% performance-per-dollar edge for GB300 in the middle of the range. That comparison uses SemiAnalysis's estimate of Google's internal TCO, not the large-buyer TCO behind the 19% result. SemiAnalysis expects the gap to close once Google's own disaggregated setup is tuned.</p>
    <p>There are no agent tests yet. SemiAnalysis promises results for agentic workloads, where an AI works through long multi-step tasks, "later in the year".</p>
    <p>Every number above comes from one model, Qwen3.5 397B.</p>
    <p>SemiAnalysis thanks Google's TPU team for the work behind these results.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section" id="trillium">Trillium, the generation before</h2>
    <ChipFigure src="/ai/hardware/chips/trillium.jpg" alt="Google Trillium TPU on its circuit board: a large silver lid around the silicon, with the word Trillium printed along the bottom edge." caption="Google Trillium (TPU v6e)." credit="Image: Google" variant="section" />
    <p>Trillium is Google's sixth-generation TPU. Google Cloud calls it TPU v6e. Google announced it on 14 May 2024 (<a href="https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/compute/introducing-trillium-6th-gen-tpus">Google Cloud, Announcing Trillium</a>) and made it generally available on 11 December 2024 (<a href="https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/compute/trillium-tpu-is-ga">Google Cloud, Trillium is GA</a>).</p>
    <p>Google says Trillium has 4.7 times the peak compute per chip of TPU v5e, the chip before it. It also says Trillium doubled the HBM capacity and bandwidth and doubled the chip-to-chip link speed. HBM, or high-bandwidth memory, is the fast memory stacked right next to the chip. Google says Trillium is over 67% more energy efficient than TPU v5e. These are Google's own claims.</p>
    <p>Google Cloud's TPU documentation lists the two chips side by side (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/tpu7x">Google Cloud, TPU7x (Ironwood)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/v6e">Google Cloud, TPU v6e</a>):</p>
    <div class="ai-model-table-wrap">
      <table class="ai-model-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Per chip, unless noted</th>
            <th scope="col">Ironwood (TPU7x)</th>
            <th scope="col">Trillium (TPU v6e)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Peak compute, BF16</td><td>2,307 TFLOPS</td><td>918 TFLOPS</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Peak compute, FP8</td><td>4,614 TFLOPS</td><td>918 TFLOPS</td></tr>
          <tr><td>HBM capacity</td><td>192 GiB</td><td>32 GiB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>HBM bandwidth</td><td>7,380 GB/s</td><td>1,638 GB/s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>TensorCores</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Chip-to-chip links, both directions</td><td>1,200 GB/s</td><td>800 GB/s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Chips per pod</td><td>9,216</td><td>256</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>BF16 and FP8 are number formats. BF16 stores each value in 16 bits and FP8 in 8 bits. A TFLOPS is a trillion math operations per second. A TensorCore is the block inside the chip that does the matrix math. A pod is the largest group of chips Google wires together as one system. Google lists the same 918 TFLOPS for Trillium in both formats, while Ironwood doubles from BF16 to FP8.</p>
    <p>Trillium rents for less. On the same pricing page, Trillium is $2.70 per chip-hour on demand in us-east1 (South Carolina) and $1.22 on a three-year commitment (<a href="https://cloud.google.com/tpu/pricing">Google Cloud, Cloud TPU pricing</a>). Google lists Trillium in us-east1, us-east5, europe-west4, and asia-northeast1, not in us-central1, the region used for the Ironwood prices above.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">What comes next: TPU 8t and TPU 8i</h2>
    <p>Google announced its eighth-generation TPUs on 22 April 2026 at Cloud Next '26 (<a href="https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/compute/ai-infrastructure-at-next26">Google Cloud, AI infrastructure at Next '26</a>). There are two chips. TPU 8t is for training and TPU 8i is for inference (<a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/infrastructure-and-cloud/google-cloud/tpus-8t-8i-cloud-next/">Google, new TPUs at Cloud Next '26</a>). They are a new generation, not a split version of Ironwood. Ironwood is one chip that does both jobs.</p>
    <p>Google says TPU 8i delivers 80% better performance per dollar for inference than the prior generation, and that both chips "will be available to Cloud customers soon". That is Google's own claim. SemiAnalysis notes that TPU 8i adds native FP4, meaning the chip runs the 4-bit format directly.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Anthropic and TPUs</h2>
    <p>Anthropic, the company behind Claude, is a major TPU customer. Here is what it has said.</p>
    <p>On 23 October 2025, Anthropic said it would use "up to one million TPUs," an expansion "worth tens of billions of dollars" that is "expected to bring well over a gigawatt of capacity online in 2026" (<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/news/expanding-our-use-of-google-cloud-tpus-and-services">Anthropic, Expanding our use of Google Cloud TPUs and Services</a>). A gigawatt is a billion watts, a common way to size data centers by the power they draw.</p>
    <p>On 6 April 2026, Anthropic signed a new deal with Google and Broadcom for "multiple gigawatts of next-generation TPU capacity that we expect to come online starting in 2027" (<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/news/google-broadcom-partnership-compute">Anthropic, Google and Broadcom compute deal</a>). Broadcom's 8-K filing says Broadcom develops and supplies custom TPUs for Google under a long-term agreement.</p>
    <p>Broadcom's 8-K filing gives a number for Anthropic's share. An 8-K is a report a public company files with US regulators about a major event. It says Anthropic, "beginning in 2027, will access through Broadcom approximately 3.5 gigawatts" of TPU-based compute, and that Anthropic's use of it "is dependent on Anthropic's continued commercial success" (<a href="https://investors.broadcom.com/static-files/c906d370-921b-4bc2-bb7b-57877dfcf1ae">Broadcom, Form 8-K</a>).</p>
    <p>SemiAnalysis estimates that Anthropic committed to "over one million" TPUs, about 400,000 or more bought outright and 600,000 or more rented through Google Cloud. That is SemiAnalysis's estimate, not an Anthropic figure.</p>
